--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Modifications made by this project
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

1. Build system: the reference builds its client bundle with tsdown and copies
   `lib/index.iife.js`; this project builds the same sources with rollup into
   `window.__ModuleLoader__.load({ id, factory })` CJS form (tools/build-client.mjs).
2. Settings transport: DSH 0.2.1 no longer provides `ctx.settingsScope`, so
   `packages/dsh-fairy-next-visual/src/client/scope-shim.js` supplies the same
   `getSnapshot()/subscribe()/set()` surface over a host HTTP route
   (`GET/PUT /dsh-fairy/settings?ns=<namespace>`) backed by JSON files in
   `$DSH_HOME/fairy-visual/` and `$DSH_HOME/fairy-identity/`.
3. Host halves (`src/index.js`): the reference registers settings through the
   0.1.1 `settings` service; here each package registers its own `webServer`
   route instead. Voice paths and the balance-meter credentials path now honour
   `DSH_HOME` / `DSH_FAIRY_CREDENTIALS`.
4. DOM compatibility for DSH 0.2.1 in `packages/dsh-fairy-next-visual/src/client/dom-adapter.js`:
   `conversation` also matches `[data-slot="main.conversation"]`,
   `composerTextarea` also matches `[data-composer-input]` (0.2.1 uses a
   contenteditable div instead of a textarea), and `phaseActive` stays
   `[data-phase="active"]` because the composer input carries its own
   `data-phase` vocabulary (`plain`).
5. `dsh-fairy-next-startup` tolerates the missing `sessions.clear()` in 0.2.1 instead
   of failing the whole client graph.
6. Relative contract imports are rewritten to `../../../dsh-fairy-next-contracts/`.
7. Added tooling that the reference does not ship: `tools/build-client.mjs`,
   `tools/build-all.mjs`, `tools/verify-build.mjs`, `tools/port-packages.mjs`,
   `tools/dev-*.mjs`, `tools/cdp/**` (CDP parity/contrast/DOM audits),
   `dev/profile/**` (isolated dev profile).
8. Session selection source: the reference reads
   `sessions.list.getSnapshot().current`, which no longer exists in 0.2.1 (the
   snapshot only carries `ids`/`byId`/`phase`/`projectionsBySession`), so the
   handover never fired and activity stayed `normal`. `session-source.js` reads
   `uiSession.current` instead and falls back to the sessions store.
9. Model-picker stacking: the mascot animation-speed rail is an invisible
   `input[type=range]` spanning the same band as the native model button and
   swallowed every click. The reference reproduces this overlap, so the fix here
   is a stacking change only (the picker is lifted above the rail; geometry is
   untouched).
10. Model-picker ownership: the reference clones the native model control and
    hides the original (`-native-` marker). 0.2.1 exposes a single node carrying
    both markers, which the hide rule erased entirely, so a session could not
    change model. `style.js` restores that node when it is the only copy and
    anchors it to the send button (right wing, bottom row) instead of the 0.1.1
    clone slot in the left wing.
11. Header controls: the Fairy controls are placed before the stock header
    buttons (owner request) with the header's 8px rhythm, and the blank-session
    fallback toggle stops short of the 0.2.1 header-corner button instead of
    covering it.
12. Mascot preferences (`mascotScale`, `mascotAnimationSpeed`) are part of the
    stored schema; the first port dropped them on write, which silently reset the
    scale slider after every drag.
13. Header control sizing: the three Fairy header controls are tightened to read at
    the stock header height (labels kept, fonts and paddings reduced; 52/68/30 px
    wide -> 40/58/26 px), and the blank-session fallback borrows the stock corner
    button's top edge for vertical alignment. No glyphs, labels or shapes are
    changed from the reference.
14. Persona configuration replaces the reference's preset wiring. Fairy-DSH puts
    its persona into `@deepseek-ai/dsh-persona` (the 0.1.1-era `config.text`
    field) inside `.agent-presets/fairy/agent.cordis.yml` and samples its corpus
    through a Python `fairy-core.runtime`. This port instead registers its own
    `fairy:persona` prompt section (order 100, `interpolate: false`, text provider
    re-evaluated on every assembly) and exposes that text as a settings field, so
    the prompt can be edited without touching a profile. New code lives in
    `packages/dsh-fairy-next-visual/src/persona.js` (composition, identity clause,
    corpus access) plus the client-side `TtsSettings`/`PersonaSettings` blocks.
15. Redistributed configuration assets: `packages/dsh-fairy-next-visual/assets/persona/`
    reproduces the reference repository's `.agent-presets/fairy/` material —
    `fairy-persona.md` is extracted from the `text:` block of its
    `agent.cordis.yml`, and `corpus/` copies `preset.yml`, `ASSET-BOUNDARIES.json`,
    `behavior/fairy_behavior_rules.json`, `canon/fairy_canon.json`,
    `canon/fairy_anti_fanon.json`, `personality/fairy_personality.json`,
    `personality/fairy_relationships.json`, `personality/fairy_world_view.json`,
    `style/fairy_real_ai_antipatterns.json`, `style/fairy_speech_style.json` and
    `style/fairy_voice_model.json` verbatim (341 KB in total). They remain under
    the upstream Apache License 2.0 and ship as optional reference material only:
    nothing is injected unless the operator inserts it in the settings UI.
16. Local TTS configuration is new surface, not ported code: the reference reads
    its endpoint from the environment and a fixed reference-audio path. This port
    keeps those as defaults while adding a persisted override
    (`$DSH_HOME/fairy-voice/tts-config.json`), a reference-audio upload endpoint
    and a settings block.
17. The voice volume slider stays adjustable while the speech engine is
    unreachable. The reference disables both the auto-read dot and the volume
    range whenever `/fairy-voice/status` reports unavailable, which renders as a
    visible-but-dead control; here only the auto-read toggle is disabled. The
    volume is a saved preference (`dsh.fairyVoice.volume`), and the control group
    carries `data-dsh-fairy-engine-ready` plus the unavailability reason as its
    `title`, so the disabled state explains itself on hover.
18. The "模型与推理强度" popup is third-party surface, not ported code. Fairy-DSH
    installs `dsh-reasoning-effort` v0.6.2 (HanaAyane, MIT) into its profile and
    patches only the chibi sprite; its `.re-model-menu` markup and behaviour are
    that plugin's. This repository styles the same classes (menu colours, effort
    contrast) and, in the dev profile, pins the 0.2.x rewrite
    `@ayanehana/dsh-reasoning-effort@0.9.0`. That version tries to wrap the stock
    model button and matches it through a `menu.aria` dictionary key that its own
    locale files do not define, so the effort panel never appears on DSH 0.2.1;
    `tools/patch-reasoning-effort.mjs` makes it take the plugin's own
    `AdvancedModelSelect` branch instead. No plugin source is redistributed here,
    and the patch is a dev-profile convenience applied after `pnpm install`.
19. Composer model seat: the reference popup renders the model name and effort
    label because its composer row keeps a measurable inline size. This port's
    row is `display:contents`, which collapses the stock container query and
    flips the row into `data-model-compact` (icon only). The theme now restores
    `--dsh-composer-model-text-display`/`--dsh-composer-model-icon-display` on
    that row and lifts the effort label and tick contrast above 4.5:1; geometry
    (220x28 seat, ~35px gap to the send button) follows the plugin's own layout.
20. The effort slider thumb: `@ayanehana/dsh-reasoning-effort` draws a running
    "大肥鱼" sprite sheet (40x55, `animation: re-chibi-run`) whenever
    `is-chibi` is on, which is its default. Fairy-DSH's own profile patch
    replaces that sprite with a plain white knob, so this repository does the
    same by CSS only (no plugin code is modified): `.re-effort.is-chibi
    .re-effort-knob` becomes a 28x28 white circle with a violet glow, gated on
    `html[data-dsh-fairy-visual]:not([data-dsh-fairy-chibi-thumb="true"])`. The
    sprite stays available as a setting (`chibiThumb`, off by default) for users
    who prefer the plugin's own look; enabling it writes
    `data-dsh-fairy-chibi-thumb="true"` and adds `is-chibi` from a
    `MutationObserver` so the plugin's later-rendered slider is covered too.

